.TH tcptoudp "8" "November 2026" "3proxy" "Universal proxy server"
.SH NAME
.B tcptoudp
\- TCP to UDP tunnel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR "tcptoudp " [ -d ]
.IB \fR[ -l \fR[ \fR[ @ \fR] logfile \fR]]
.IB \fR[ -i internal_ip\fR]
.IB \fR[ -e external_ip\fR]
.I local_port remote_host remote_port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tcptoudp
takes TCP connections on
.I local_port
and sends what they carry as datagrams to
.IR remote_host : remote_port ,
framing each datagram with its length on the connection. This is the
server side of udptunnel (the rfc1036 one, started with \fB-s\fR) and of
ProxyGuard (eduVPN), whose clients it answers: a connection that begins
with udptunnel's 32 byte handshake has it taken off, one that asks with
an HTTP Upgrade for UoTLV/1 is told the protocols are switched, and one
that begins with a length goes to the framing at once. What comes back
from the target is sent on the connection framed the same way. Each
connection has a UDP socket of its own to the target; a parent the ACL
gives is a SOCKS5 UDP relay, as for udppm.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -I
Inetd mode. Standalone service only.
.TP
.B -d
Daemonize. Detach service from console and run in the background.
.TP
.B -t
Be silenT. Do not log start/stop/accept error records.
.TP
.B -e
External address. IP address of the interface the proxy should send datagrams
from.
By default, the system will decide which address to use in accordance
with the routing table.
.TP
.B \-ni\fIPATH\fR
(Linux only) Switch to the network namespace identified by
.I PATH
before opening the listening socket. The current namespace is saved and restored
immediately after binding, so outgoing datagrams run in the original namespace
unless
.B \-ne
is also given.
.TP
.B \-ne\fIPATH\fR
(Linux only) Switch to the network namespace identified by
.I PATH
after the listening socket has been bound (and after restoring from
.B \-ni
if applicable). Both options accept any namespace file path
(e.g.\& \fI/var/run/netns/myns\fR or \fI/proc/PID/ns/net\fR)
and require \fBCAP_SYS_ADMIN\fR.
.TP
.B -i
Internal address. IP address the proxy accepts connections to.
By default, connections to any interface are accepted. It\'s usually unsafe.
.TP
.B -l
Log. By default logging is to stdout. If
.I logfile
is specified logging is to file. Under Unix, if
.RI \' @ \'
precedes
.IR logfile ,
syslog is used for logging.
.TP
.B -S
Increase or decrease stack size. You may want to try something like -S8192 if you experience 3proxy
crashes.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I local_port
- port tcptoudp accepts connections on
.TP
.I remote_host
- IP address of the host datagrams are sent to
.TP
.I remote_port
- remote port datagrams are sent to
.SH CLIENTS
udptunnel's client (without \fB-s\fR), ProxyGuard's client
(\fBproxyguard-client\fR), or anything that puts datagrams on a TCP
connection with a two byte length in network order in front of each.
